however, based on later messages, it seems like you think that a crate with code like this is fine to publish on crates.io and should be considered well-behaved and sound
```rust
pub fn totally_safe_fn() {
  breakpoint();
  unsafe { hint::unreachable_unchecked() };
}
```
My take on this program is that it is unsound *for some inputs*. It's okay to have an unsound program while debugging locally (chances are you are debugging it because it's already unsound somewhere else). the program is only unsound if you actually continue, and if you dont continue form that breakpoint and weird stuff happens that's definitely a compiler bug (or a bug in some unsafe code somewhere else). If all you want is a guarantee that you can debug such a program and get sensible behavior as long as you dont continue, we have no disagreements and I think yeet2 can serve that purpose. But published code is by default assumed to be sound for all inputs so I would file a ticket for a crate with a public safe function like the above.

If what you want is that I can have code like the above in published crates, i.e., when seeing `breakpoint()` in other people's code I must default to assuming that this is `halting_breakpoint()`, then we do indeed disagree. I would say that `abort_immediate()` should be able to serve most of the situations where one wants a "breakpoint that halts execution explosively" -- assuming debuggers actually make that work for whatever abort_immediate expands to; I dont know whether it does. The only usecase not covered by this is "breakpoint that halts execution explosively but I want a way to continue the program anyway" which seems like it would be extremely niche? is the space not covered by abort_immediate + resumable_breakpoint (including local use or use locally in a team where you make a deal with everyone not to continue after breakpoints, which the compiler is required to respect) so big that we need anther operation, namely yeet1, to fill it? (and cannot have resumable_breakpoint without having yeet1 as well)

