I don't know how your model works exactly; I had the following mental model of how it would work (but this doesn't work, see below):

The asm block comes with (a) a single assembly implementation and (b) a (finite?) set of stories. In the AM execution, when we hit an asm block, the angel pick one of its stories. We run this story in the AM, the assembly in the CM. If in the AM we hit UB then the angel picked the wrong story (we could think of this as having to backtrack, or just as this not being a "valid" choice for the angel). If not, we check that (i) the final AM state maps to the final CM state (in the AM to CM mapping), and (ii) the IO/observable behaviour trace of the AM and CM executions coincide. **If not, then the angel picked the wrong story (backtrack).** Then we continue execution on the AM, if we hit UB, again backtrack.

If the angel has a valid choice then the execution chooses such valid choice. If not, then the program hits UB (before even entering the asm?).

This model allows you to implement `freeze`, but it is way too powerful and breaks all optimisations. Take the following asm block: input `addr: usize`, output `y: bool`, assembly performs a load from `addr`, stories `true` and `false`. Now pass this assembly block the address of a non-exposed `bool`.

```rust
fn bad(addr: usize) -> bool {
    let y: u8;
    // stories: `true`, `false`
    unsafe {
        std::arch::asm!(
            "mov {1}, byte ptr [{0}]",
            in(reg) addr,
            out(reg_byte) y
        )
    };
    unsafe { std::mem::transmute(y) }
}

fn main() {
    let b: bool = pick();
    let addr = (&b as *const bool).addr();
    assert_eq!(b, bad(addr)); // never panicks
}
```

Neither story hits UB. Neither the stories not the assembly produce any observable behaviour. Only one of the stories final CM state matches the final AM state, namely the one where the register for `y` contains the same value as the place of `b`. But the place `b` was unexposed; simply passing its address should not allow the assembly to read from `b`!

We forced the angels hand: it had to choose the story where we obtained the value of `b`, because otherwise it wouldn't match our assembly. And on the AM story side there was never a read, so no UB. We don't want to rule that if the CM execution does a load, then this should be matched in the AM (story); we already justify certain asm blocks by saying "yeah, the asm performs a load from memory it is not allowed to read, but we just story tell that it produces a random byte".

You could fix this by replacing the bold part of my mental model with "that is UB, i.e. entering the asm block is UB and you don't get an angelic choice". But then I don't think you can justify an asm block performing freeze anymore. It just becomes a hugely complicated model that doesn't seem to add much.

