Sorry, I didn't read this full thread, but let me add 2 notes.

**1** Even if Rust lacks `freeze()`, `asm!` can still perform "freeze" if it **knows which bytes are uninitialized**, because then clearly you can storytell that you did overwrite all uninit bytes with random initialized bytes. And an `asm!` block knows all input-output to the program until that point (i.e. everything passed to or returned from `asm!` and volatile memory ops), so in many cases it will know.

**2** If Rust lacks `freeze()` you cannot assume `asm!` can perform freeze; doing so could actually cause miscompilations. Take for example the following code:
```rust
// Implements freeze
fn f(mut x: MaybeUninit<u8>) -> u8 {
    unsafe {
        asm!("/* {} */", inout(reg_byte) x);
        x.assume_init()
    }
}

fn main() {
    let b: bool = pick();
    let mut x = MaybeUninit::<u8>::uninit();
    if b {
        x.write(37);
    }

    let y = f(x);

    if b {
        assert_eq!(y, 37);
    }
}
```
Now I, the optimiser, will reason as follows. I don't want to inspect the implementation of `f`, but it is an arbitrary Rust function. In particular, if I choose `b` to be `false`, then either `f` hits UB or the output `y` must be fully independent of `x` (I can prove this for any Rust program). If `f` hits UB I can do anything, so assume the latter. That means that regardless of `b`, I can pass uninit to `f`; it doesn't matter for its execution. So the write to `x` is dead. Now pick `b` to be `true`. I obtain:
```rust
fn main() {
    let y = f(MaybeUninit::uninit());
    assert_eq!(y, 37);
}
```
But that program panicks, even though the original was supposed not to panick (the `asm!` was supposed to implement freeze).

