We've discussed [this](https://github.com/rust-lang/unsafe-code-guidelines/issues/259) before... the case of having statics that mark the beginning and end of a memory range that's filly by :magic_wand: linker magic :sparkles:  and that we iterate from Rust. iteration is not hard but you have to be careful as END is a separate allocation from START:
```rust
// Access a "linker section slice" spanned between `whatever.start` and `whatever.end`.
// Assumption: both are usize-aligned and there's an implicit list of usizes stored between them.
unsafe extern "C" {
  #[link_name = "whatever.start"]
  static START: [usize; 0];
  #[link_name = "whatever.end"]
  static END: [usize; 0];
}

/// Call the callback on each element of the "linker section slice".
#[allow(static_mut_refs)] // we immediately decay them to raw pointers
pub fn iter(mut callback: impl FnMut(usize)) { unsafe {
  let mut ptr = START.as_ptr();
  while ptr.addr() != END.as_ptr().addr() {
    callback(ptr.read());
    ptr = ptr.add(1);
  }
} }
```
But @**Josh Triplett** raises the question of what happens when you also want to put stuff into that linker section from the same program. That would look roughly like
```rust
#[link_section = ".whatever"]
#[used]
static MY_PART: usize = 15; // can also be an array of `usize`
```
Now if we do that we have technically created overlapping slices -- we are using START to access memory in MY_PART. @**Nikita Popov** said LLVM isn't happy about overlapping statics. Is there any way we can bless this pattern? Notably, all involved memory is read-only so LLVM can still assume `noalias` between all involved pointers. but it can't make assumptions about their addresses, that would go wrong.

