messages.en:
Could we have an opt-in to a stricter version that inserts the calls to `restrict_provenance` in crates that want this automatically? Actually assuming that references from function arguments come with a restricted provenance would not be okay, sure, but at least such code would not hand out much larger prov to code it calls into. Without having to manually attach a call to `restrict_provenance` in basically all places where a reference is formed.
This wouldn't be quite as much as supporting both code with and without subobject references side-by-side and even less for switching to full subobject references for all (except extern types perhaps).

The reason is I allocate a larger array of some type up front and allocate from that. I would want miri to catch me using a reference to one index to refer to another index somewhere else. "Protected" tags do only get placed on the "restricted" portion of a mutable reference, if I read that right in Tree Borrows.

Even so, the two functions below *should* be equivalent (teaching that they are not would be hard for me at least), since `opque_fun` is seemingly a safe function to call.

```
fn foo(x: &mut Foo) {
  x.a = 1;
  opaque_fun(&mut x.b);
  assert!(x.a == 1);
}
fn foo(x: &mut Foo) {
  let x_a = &mut x.a;
  *x_a = 1;
  opaque_fun(&mut org.b);
  assert!(*x_a == 1);
}
```

messages.zh:
我们能否提供一种可选择启用的更严格版本：对于希望采用该版本的 crate，自动插入对 `restrict_provenance` 的调用？当然，假设函数参数传入的引用都带有受限的 provenance 肯定不行，但至少这样一来，这类代码不会把大得多的 provenance 交给它所调用的代码。这样就不必在几乎所有形成引用的地方手动附加对 `restrict_provenance` 的调用了。

这还不如同时支持带子对象引用和不带子对象引用的代码，更不用说切换到对所有对象都使用完整的子对象引用了（或许 extern 类型除外）。

原因是，我会预先分配一个较大的某种类型的数组，然后从中进行分配。我希望 miri 能捕捉到这样的错误：在某处使用指向一个索引的引用去指代另一个索引。如果我的 Tree Borrows 理解无误，那么“受保护”的标签只会放在可变引用中“受限”的那一部分上。

即便如此，下面两个函数*应该*是等价的（至少对我来说，要让我相信它们不等价会很困难），因为 `opque_fun` 看起来是一个可以安全调用的函数。

```rust
fn foo(x: &mut Foo) {
  x.a = 1;
  opaque_fun(&mut x.b);
  assert!(x.a == 1);
}
fn foo(x: &mut Foo) {
  let x_a = &mut x.a;
  *x_a = 1;
  opaque_fun(&mut org.b);
  assert!(*x_a == 1);
}
```

messages.en:
@_**Mario Carneiro|271719** [said](https://rust-lang.zulipchat.com/#narrow/channel/136281-t-opsem/topic/Vibes.20on.20subobject.20provenance/near/607833205):
```quote
I don't think this is a hard model to teach
```

if you start with "only what safe Rust can do" strongly typed intuition, sure.
if you start with "raw system access" it's a surprising limitation, as witnessed by all the code out there that breaks this model. again, this is somewhere up there in the top 3 or so for why code has UB. (hard to give exact numbers, someone would have to do more precise data gathering.)

The biggest thing I dislike about losing (or never having) subobject provenance is that regular Rust that wants type-wise obvious optimisations has to jump through odd hoops to guarantee them:

```rust
// Returns a nice Foo and performs thread-safe work on foreign Bar, otherwise returns an Error.
fn foo_is_nice(&self) -> Result<Nice, Error> {
  if !self.foo.is_nice() {
    return Error(..);
  }
  self.bar_mutex.lock().unwrap().work();
  Ok(self.foo.as_nice())
}
```
That `as_nice` will probably have to redo the `is_nice` check and add some panic branch for if the caller was wrong about it being nice, because the `Mutex<Bar>` work in between may have subobjected its way into `Foo` and changed it to be not nice.

So you do something like this:
```rust
let foo_ref = &self.foo;
if !foo_ref.is_nice() {
  return Err(..);
}
self.bar_mutex.lock().unwrap().work();
// Note: the reference being live until after the work makes it UB for work to touch Foo, and therefore gives Rust the permission to ignore the `!self.is_nice()` panic path inside `as_nice()`
let _ = foo_ref;
Ok(self.foo.as_nice())
```

And maybe that too isn't actually enough and you need to actually insert a read through `foo_ref` so that it's not considered unused and deleted? I'm not quite sure on that.

Looking at this all from a C++ interop perspective, not having subobject provenance would definitely make it easier to do some stuff (eg. I could go from `&Base` to get a virtual method and it's derived class offset from the vtable, then convert the `&Base` into a `&()` and call the vtable method with that as `this`), but on the JavaScript engine front on the other hand I am a little apprehensive about having to think about the lack of subobject provenance and how to get it back when I'm writing pure, safe Rust.

messages.zh:
我最不喜欢失去（或从未拥有）子对象来源的一点是：那些希望从类型层面获得显而易见优化的普通 Rust 代码，必须绕一些奇怪的弯来保证这些优化：

```rust
// 返回一个不错的 Foo，并对外部的 Bar 执行线程安全的操作，否则返回一个 Error。
fn foo_is_nice(&self) -> Result<Nice, Error> {
  if !self.foo.is_nice() {
    return Error(..);
  }
  self.bar_mutex.lock().unwrap().work();
  Ok(self.foo.as_nice())
}
```

`as_nice` 可能必须重新执行 `is_nice` 检查，并为调用者误判其为 nice 的情况增加一个 panic 分支，因为中间对 `Mutex<Bar>` 的操作可能通过子对象的方式影响了 `Foo`，把它改成不再 nice。

所以你可能得这样做：
```rust
let foo_ref = &self.foo;
if !foo_ref.is_nice() {
  return Err(..);
}
self.bar_mutex.lock().unwrap().work();
// 注意：该引用一直存活到这次操作之后，这会使 work 接触 Foo 成为 UB，因此 Rust 有权忽略 `as_nice()` 内部针对 `!self.is_nice()` 的 panic 路径。
let _ = foo_ref;
Ok(self.foo.as_nice())
```

而且，也许这样其实还不够，你还需要实际通过 `foo_ref` 进行一次读取，这样它才不会被视为未使用并被删除？这点我不太确定。

从 C++ 互操作的角度来看，不具备子对象来源肯定会让某些事情更容易实现（例如，我可以从 `&Base` 获取虚方法及其派生类在虚函数表中的偏移量，然后把 `&Base` 转换为 `&()`，再用它作为 `this` 调用虚函数表中的方法）；但另一方面，从 JavaScript 引擎的角度来看，如果我在编写纯粹的安全 Rust，就必须考虑缺少子对象来源的问题，以及如何把它恢复回来，这让我有些担忧。

