Here you go.

**Theorem.**
In any "reasonable"* semantics, you cannot have all of the following:
1. `let _ = ptr.addr();` can be optimized away
2. `let ptr = alloc(); dealloc(ptr);` can be optimized away
3. integers do not carry provenance
4. `ptr1 == ptr2` is equivalent to `ptr1.addr() == ptr2.addr()` (assuming thin pointers)
5. `if ptr1 == ptr2 { assert_eq!(*ptr1, *ptr2); }` never panicks (unless UB)
6. a fully safe program cannot hit UB

*: You need all kinds of very reasonable properties for your opsem, e.g. writing to one allocation shouldn't change the contents of another allocation, and comparison of integers should be side-effect free.

*Proof sketch:*

```rust
// any number at least 2^61 (on 64-bit target) or 2^30 (on 32-bit target)
const N: usize = isize::MAX as usize;
type T = Box<usize>;

// only safe code, so by (6), no UB occurs
// try a small number for `N` to see that everything compiles on stable (borrow checker is happy)
fn source() {
    // this doesn't compile with
    // `error: values of the type [...] are too big for the target architecture`
    // but you could get around that by using many smaller arrays (or `Vec`s) instead
    let mut boxes = [const { None::<T> } ; N];

    // allocate `N` boxes
    for i in 0..N {
        boxes[i] = Some(Box::new(i));
    }

    // enumerate all pairs of indices (`i`, `j`) where `i != j`
    for j in 0..N {
        for i in 0..j {
            // note that the unwrap never panicks
            let ref_i: &usize = boxes[i].as_ref().unwrap();
            let ref_j: &usize = boxes[j].as_ref().unwrap();
            // by (3) there are at most 2^64 `usize` values, of which only 2^61 - 1 are
            // 8-byte aligned and nonnull
            // I have `N` > 2^61 - 1 many boxes, so two boxes share their address
            // by (4) this compares address, so the branch is taken at least once
            if (ref_i as *const usize) == (ref_j as *const usize) {
                // we know that `*ref_i == i` and `*ref_j == j` and `i != j`
                // so the assert is panicks (contradicting (5))
                assert_eq!(*ref_i, *ref_j);
            }
        }
    }

    // drop the boxes in reversed order
    for i in (0..N).rev() {
        boxes[i] = None;
    }
}
// so this program is guaranteed to panick/abort

fn intermediate1() {
    let mut boxes = [const { None::<T> } ; N];
    for i in 0..N {
        // the write to `boxes` is dead so can be optimised out
        let _ = Some(Box::new(i));
    }

    // by (5) the `assert_eq` can be optimised out
    // then by (1) and since integer comparison is side-effect-free the entire `if` can
    // be optimised out
    // then the indexing in `boxes` + `unwrap()` can be optimised out
    // then the empty double `loop` can be optimised out

    for i in (0..N).rev() {
        boxes[i] = None;
    }
}

fn intermediate2() {
    let mut boxes = [const { None::<T> } ; N];
    //for i in 0..N {
        // the write to the box is dead and can be optimised out
        // then the allocation with `i` = `N - 1` is immediately followed by the drop/dealloc
        // so by (2) the alloc-dealloc pair can be optimised out
        // repeat the argument for `i` = `N - 2`, `N - 3`, ..., `0`
        // and then the entire loop can be optimised out
