I think the only options we could reasonably compromise on are (3) and (4). Maybe we didn't discuss compromising on (3) (integers don't carry provenance) enough as a viable option. Max proposed something like that

@_**Max|960514** [said](https://rust-lang.zulipchat.com/#narrow/channel/136281-t-opsem/topic/Do.20we.20already.20have.20overlapping.20allocations.3F.20.28OOM.20Edition.29/near/621878788):

> i.e. all reasoning on addresses and allocation sizes occurs in the natural numbers instead

and there is https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/3674652, but I think the nicest model in this direction would be to have planes just like in the other proposal, but make `usize` (and other numeric types?) values carry a plane as well. Numeric literals like `37` evaluate to a value in the zero plane, equality for `usize` would take both the number and the plane into account, and operations like addition could either function pointwise or always the the plane of the first input as the plane for the output. You get some strange behaviour though:
```rust
let addr = alloc().addr();
for n in 0..=usize::MAX {
    if n == addr { println!("yes"); }
}
```
might not print anything...

I definitely prefer compromising on (4) though; seems like a cleaner and less surprising semantics.

