That `write`/`read` combo would only be UB in some cases, and when it's not UB it would be a correct optimization to remove it
```

Oh, you only have UB on the integer decode if the plane is non-zero. Then you can't hoist integer load across `.addr()` which sounds pretty bad; i.e. this transformation is not allowed:
```rust
fn f_source(uniq: &mut u8, intref: &usize) {
    let uniq_ptr: *mut _ = uniq;
    let _ = uniq_ptr.addr();
    let _int = *intref;
}

fn f_target(uniq: &mut u8, intref: &usize) {
    let _int = *intref;
    let uniq_ptr: *mut _ = uniq;
    let _ = uniq_ptr.addr();
}
```
[playground](https://play.rust-lang.org/?version=stable&mode=debug&edition=2024&gist=622d985f3de08e7d97310c128281b2ca)

The reason is that you can call `f` in a way where `uniq` is stored behind the reference `intref`, so `uniq.addr()` before the load from `intref` would guarantee that `intref` points at a zero-plane pointer (which can be decoded as integer without UB).

I think that is pretty bad.

