I was under the impression that the Rust AM considers FFI and ASM in the same way (i.e. they don't really exist, so to say). I was also under the impression that the current belief around ASM is to follow the "storytelling" approach, under which I believe the `copy_with_inline_asm` function in the code block below (LLM generated) would *not* be UB (because for the AM it just wrote _some_ bytes into dst).

However, miri complains about copy_with_libc_memcpy, which given my beliefs above about FFI is a bit surprising. Is that because libc::memcpy is "special" in some way? Am I misunderstanding how the AM treats FFI?

```
use std::arch::asm;
use std::ffi::c_void;
use std::mem::MaybeUninit;

/// 1. Copies 64 bytes of uninitialized memory using `libc::memcpy`
fn copy_with_libc_memcpy() {
    // Allocate 64 bytes of uninitialized stack memory
    let src = MaybeUninit::<[u8; 64]>::uninit();
    let mut dst = [0u8; 64];

    unsafe {
        libc::memcpy(
            dst.as_mut_ptr() as *mut c_void,
            src.as_ptr() as *const c_void,
            dst.len(),
        );
    }

    println!("libc::memcpy bytes:\n{:02x?}\n", dst);
}

/// 2. Copies 64 bytes of uninitialized memory using x86-64 inline assembly
#[cfg(target_arch = "x86_64")]
fn copy_with_inline_asm() {
    // Allocate 64 bytes of uninitialized stack memory
    let src = MaybeUninit::<[u8; 64]>::uninit();
    let mut dst = [0u8; 64];

    unsafe {
        // `rep movsb` copies `rcx` bytes from `[rsi]` to `[rdi]`.
        // Because `rep movsb` mutates `rdi`, `rsi`, and `rcx`, they MUST
        // be declared as `inout` with discarded outputs (`=> _`).
        asm!(
            "rep movsb",
            inout("rdi") dst.as_mut_ptr() => _,
            inout("rsi") src.as_ptr() => _,
            inout("rcx") dst.len() => _,
            options(nostack, preserves_flags)
        );
    }

    println!("x86_64 inline asm bytes:\n{:02x?}", dst);
}

fn main() {
    copy_with_libc_memcpy();

    #[cfg(target_arch = "x86_64")]
    copy_with_inline_asm();
}
```

