messages.en:
You don't need to hand-roll a memcpy. In your example you could do
```rust
/// 3. Copies 64 bytes of uninitialized memory using `libc::memcpy` and an empty asm block
fn copy_with_libc_memcpy_and_asm() {
    // Allocate 64 bytes of uninitialized stack memory
    let src = MaybeUninit::<[u8; 64]>::uninit();
    let mut dst = [0u8; 64];

    // perform the actual copy, preserves uninitness
    unsafe {
        libc::memcpy(
            dst.as_mut_ptr() as *mut c_void,
            src.as_ptr() as *const c_void,
            dst.len(),
        );
    }
    // storytell that this overwrites `dst` with random *initialized* bytes
    unsafe {
        asm!(
            "/* {} */",
            in(reg) dst.as_mut_ptr(),
            options(nostack, preserves_flags)
        );
    }

    println!("libc::memcpy bytes:\n{:02x?}\n", dst);
}
```

Note that Rust doesn't have [`freeze` yet](https://github.com/rust-lang/rfcs/pull/4001), so if `src` contains some initialised bytes, *and you need to preserve their value* then you are out of luck currently (with any approach).

