messages.en:
So with that out of the way...
```rust
loop {
  let x = Box::into_raw(Box::new(0i32));
  let y = Box::into_raw(Box::new(1i32));
  if x.expose_provenance() != y.expose_provenance() { continue; }

  // Now we have two allocations with the same address.
  let ptr = ptr::with_exposed_provenance(x.addr());
  // Which provenance did it pick? Who knows...
  if unsafe { ptr.read() } != 0 {
    unsafe { hint::unreachable_unchecked() };
  }
}
```
The angelic choice means we are *forced* to pick the provenance of x to avoid the UB. But that UB happens later in the program! We can use this to 'predict' pretty much arbitrarily complicated computations...

This is not really a problem because in practice this program will loop forever. IOW, every program still has a 'trivial' refinement where allocations never overlap and therefore provenance cannot affect the actual values in the program and therefore we dont have to predict the future. But I thought it was a fun observation :)

